 SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) – Know What's Inside Your Software 📋

🔹 What is an SBOM?
A Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) is a detailed inventory of all the components, libraries, frameworks, and dependencies used to build an application.
Think of it like an ingredient list on a food package—it tells you exactly what's inside your software.

🔑 What Does an SBOM Include?
✔ Application name & version
 ✔ Open-source libraries
 ✔ Third-party dependencies
 ✔ Component versions
 ✔ License information

🌟 Why is SBOM Important?
Without an SBOM:
❌ Unknown vulnerable dependencies
 ❌ Difficult vulnerability management
 ❌ Compliance challenges

With an SBOM:
✔ Quickly identify vulnerable components 🔍
 ✔ Improve software transparency 📋
 ✔ Simplify compliance and audits ✅
 ✔ Strengthen software supply chain security 🔒

💡 Real-world Example
A new vulnerability is discovered in a popular open-source library.
Instead of manually checking every application, the security team reviews the SBOM and immediately identifies which applications use the affected version.
✅ The vulnerable library is updated before it can be exploited.

🧠 Key Takeaways
✔ SBOM = Inventory of software components 📋
 ✔ Lists all dependencies and versions 📦
 ✔ Helps identify vulnerable libraries 🔍
 ✔ Improves compliance and security 🔒
 ✔ Essential for modern DevSecOps 🚀
